1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,435
One molecule of glucose yields two of
carbon dioxide, yet the loaf stays flat.

2
00:00:05,435 --> 00:00:09,673
Bread fails to rise for one of two
physical reasons: the yeast is not

3
00:00:09,673 --> 00:00:14,709
producing enough carbon dioxide, or the
dough cannot hold the gas it does produce.

4
00:00:14,709 --> 00:00:17,289
The gas itself follows a fixed recipe.

5
00:00:17,289 --> 00:00:22,246
In yeast fermentation, as chemistry
references from the American Chemical

6
00:00:22,246 --> 00:00:27,406
Society set it out, one molecule of
glucose becomes two molecules of ethanol

7
00:00:27,406 --> 00:00:29,851
and two molecules of carbon dioxide.

8
00:00:29,851 --> 00:00:34,261
That figure tells you the gas gets made,
even when the fermenter is alive and well.

9
00:00:34,261 --> 00:00:39,322
It does not tell you where the gas goes,
and that is where many flat loaves are

10
00:00:39,322 --> 00:00:40,219
actually lost.

11
00:00:40,219 --> 00:00:42,176
Why is my bread not rising?

12
00:00:42,176 --> 00:00:45,473
Start with the bubble, because the obvious
picture is wrong.

13
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:49,758
It is natural to imagine yeast cells
blowing brand new bubbles into the dough,

14
00:00:49,758 --> 00:00:52,176
like tiny balloons appearing out of
nowhere.

15
00:00:52,176 --> 00:00:56,378
Cereal chemists who study dough structure
describe something different.

16
00:00:56,378 --> 00:01:00,757
Carbon dioxide from the yeast dissolves
first into the water of the dough.

17
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:05,929
Once that water is saturated, the gas has
to go somewhere, and it diffuses into gas

18
00:01:05,929 --> 00:01:11,163
cells that already exist: small pockets of
air folded in during mixing and kneading.

19
00:01:11,163 --> 00:01:14,103
Yeast is poor at creating a bubble from
nothing.

20
00:01:14,103 --> 00:01:17,472
It is very good at inflating one that is
already there.

21
00:01:17,472 --> 00:01:22,157
Slice through a proofing dough and what
you see is gas cells around a gluten

22
00:01:22,157 --> 00:01:22,651
network.

23
00:01:22,651 --> 00:01:27,911
That network is a web of hydrated wheat
proteins, and as each gas cell swells, the

24
00:01:27,911 --> 00:01:29,451
web around it stretches.

25
00:01:29,451 --> 00:01:34,874
Rising, then, is two events stacked on top
of each other: gas being made, and gas

26
00:01:34,874 --> 00:01:35,611
being kept.

27
00:01:35,611 --> 00:01:40,235
A failure in either one gives you the same
flat dough sitting in the bowl, which is

28
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:42,965
why the symptom alone rarely tells you the
cause.

29
00:01:42,965 --> 00:01:47,305
Everything that follows sorts into one of
those two halves, and the useful habit is

30
00:01:47,305 --> 00:01:50,651
to ask which half you are looking at
before you change anything.

31
00:01:50,651 --> 00:01:52,800
Here is where the sugar comes from.

32
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,589
Flour is mostly starch, and yeast cannot
ferment the starch in flour directly.

33
00:01:57,589 --> 00:02:02,509
Amylase enzymes, some naturally present in
the grain and some added by millers, cut

34
00:02:02,509 --> 00:02:05,413
starch into simple sugars that yeast can
take up.

35
00:02:05,413 --> 00:02:09,909
Starch granules damaged during milling are
easier for those enzymes to reach.

36
00:02:09,909 --> 00:02:15,460
Only then does yeast fermentation begin,
turning those sugars into carbon dioxide

37
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:16,283
and ethanol.

38
00:02:16,283 --> 00:02:21,421
So part of the fuel supply for your rise
is decided before the bag of flour is ever

39
00:02:21,421 --> 00:02:23,712
opened, by the grain and by the mill.

40
00:02:23,712 --> 00:02:29,671
Inside the yeast cell, the species bakers
use is Saccharomyces cerevisiae, sugar is

41
00:02:29,671 --> 00:02:32,112
broken down by a chain of enzymes.

42
00:02:32,112 --> 00:02:36,472
When oxygen runs short, which happens
quickly inside a dense dough, the cell

43
00:02:36,472 --> 00:02:39,456
routes that chain toward ethanol and
carbon dioxide.

44
00:02:39,456 --> 00:02:44,845
Fermentation, in this strict sense, means
converting sugar to carbon dioxide and

45
00:02:44,845 --> 00:02:46,395
ethanol without oxygen.

46
00:02:46,395 --> 00:02:51,122
Fermentation is not the same as yeast
growth; a dough can ferment vigorously

47
00:02:51,122 --> 00:02:53,237
while its yeast barely multiplies.

48
00:02:53,237 --> 00:02:58,561
That fermentation is the work of a living
organism was established by Louis Pasteur,

49
00:02:58,561 --> 00:03:03,568
whose research the Institut Pasteur, the
institute bearing his name, documents.

50
00:03:03,568 --> 00:03:08,213
Later, Eduard Buchner showed that juice
pressed from yeast, with no intact cells

51
00:03:08,213 --> 00:03:10,187
at all, could still ferment sugar.

52
00:03:10,187 --> 00:03:13,437
That result matters to a baker for a quiet
reason.

53
00:03:13,437 --> 00:03:18,769
The gas comes from enzymes, and enzymes
are sensitive to temperature, to salt, and

54
00:03:18,769 --> 00:03:20,784
to how much water is available.

55
00:03:20,784 --> 00:03:24,475
I like questions where the obvious answer
starts falling apart once you look

56
00:03:24,475 --> 00:03:24,864
closely.

57
00:03:24,864 --> 00:03:29,712
The first group of failures is about the
fermenter: what stops the gas being made.

58
00:03:29,712 --> 00:03:32,160
Yeast killed by hot water is the classic
case.

59
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:36,682
Published thermal limits for baker's yeast
vary with the strain and with how long the

60
00:03:36,682 --> 00:03:41,045
cells are held at temperature, so there is
no single safe line worth quoting here.

61
00:03:41,045 --> 00:03:46,593
What is consistent is the direction: water
that feels hot rather than warm is in the

62
00:03:46,593 --> 00:03:48,508
range that damages the cells.

63
00:03:48,508 --> 00:03:52,933
Dead yeast produces nothing, and no amount
of waiting changes that.

64
00:03:52,933 --> 00:03:54,908
The opposite failure is quieter.

65
00:03:54,908 --> 00:03:59,413
A dough kept too cold is not dead; its
enzymes are simply working slowly.

66
00:03:59,413 --> 00:04:03,594
Bakers use this deliberately, retarding
dough in a refrigerator to stretch

67
00:04:03,594 --> 00:04:06,928
fermentation over a much longer period and
develop flavour.

68
00:04:06,928 --> 00:04:12,288
In a cool kitchen, the same slowing can
look like failure when it is only delay.

69
00:04:12,288 --> 00:04:13,883
The test here is patience.

70
00:04:13,883 --> 00:04:18,299
A cold dough with living yeast will
eventually move, and if it does, the

71
00:04:18,299 --> 00:04:20,261
fermenter was never the problem.

72
00:04:20,261 --> 00:04:23,502
Salt or sugar drawing water away is the
third failure.

73
00:04:23,502 --> 00:04:28,182
Both dissolve in the dough's water and
raise its osmotic pressure, which pulls

74
00:04:28,182 --> 00:04:29,923
water out of the yeast cells.

75
00:04:29,923 --> 00:04:35,074
In a normal recipe, salt in dough is a
flavour and a gluten strengthener, and the

76
00:04:35,074 --> 00:04:35,837
yeast copes.

77
00:04:35,837 --> 00:04:41,108
Poured directly onto the yeast, or added
in excess, it is not harmless to yeast at

78
00:04:41,108 --> 00:04:42,008
close contact.

79
00:04:42,008 --> 00:04:46,945
Rich doughs heavy with sugar are known to
rise slowly for the same osmotic reason,

80
00:04:46,945 --> 00:04:50,677
and some yeast strains are sold
specifically for sweet doughs.

81
00:04:50,677 --> 00:04:55,792
Finally, yeast weakened by age is an open
case rather than a settled one.

82
00:04:55,792 --> 00:05:00,665
Dried yeast loses activity in storage, but
how fast depends on packaging, moisture

83
00:05:00,665 --> 00:05:05,717
and temperature, and the date printed on a
packet is a manufacturer's estimate, not a

84
00:05:05,717 --> 00:05:08,213
measurement of the yeast in your cupboard.

85
00:05:08,213 --> 00:05:13,188
The direct check is the old one: stir a
little into warm water with a pinch of

86
00:05:13,188 --> 00:05:15,293
sugar and watch whether it foams.

87
00:05:15,293 --> 00:05:19,056
That small test separates a dead fermenter
from a slow one.

88
00:05:19,056 --> 00:05:22,786
Now the second group: gas that is made but
not kept.

89
00:05:22,786 --> 00:05:25,440
This is how the gluten network forms.

90
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:29,852
Wheat flour contains two families of
protein, and water hydrates gliadin and

91
00:05:29,852 --> 00:05:31,941
glutenin as soon as it reaches them.

92
00:05:31,941 --> 00:05:34,749
Then kneading aligns and links the
proteins.

93
00:05:34,749 --> 00:05:39,598
The glutenins join into long elastic
chains, while the gliadins let the mass

94
00:05:39,598 --> 00:05:40,619
flow and extend.

95
00:05:40,619 --> 00:05:42,239
Together they form gluten.

96
00:05:42,239 --> 00:05:47,224
In a well-developed dough, the network
stretches around gas cells in thin films,

97
00:05:47,224 --> 00:05:51,461
like the skin of a balloon, so each cell
swells instead of bursting.

98
00:05:51,461 --> 00:05:55,605
Weak or torn gluten lets gas escape, and
it has several sources.

99
00:05:55,605 --> 00:05:58,491
Too little kneading leaves the proteins
unaligned.

100
00:05:58,491 --> 00:06:02,704
Flour from lower-protein wheat simply
supplies less network to work with.

101
00:06:02,704 --> 00:06:06,946
Whole wheat flour carries bran, and those
sharp particles cut through the gluten

102
00:06:06,946 --> 00:06:07,264
films.

103
00:06:07,264 --> 00:06:12,185
Flours from rye, barley or oats behave
differently again, because their proteins

104
00:06:12,185 --> 00:06:16,676
do not form the same elastic web, so they
never rise like wheat even when

105
00:06:16,676 --> 00:06:18,213
fermentation is vigorous.

106
00:06:18,213 --> 00:06:22,064
A dense loaf made from those grains is
often not a failure at all.

107
00:06:22,064 --> 00:06:23,989
It is the chemistry of the grain.

108
00:06:23,989 --> 00:06:28,551
This is why protein quality is measured
rather than assumed, and it is worth

109
00:06:28,551 --> 00:06:30,352
knowing who measures the rise.

110
00:06:30,352 --> 00:06:35,940
The USDA Agricultural Research Service
runs wheat quality laboratories that mill

111
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:41,109
grain samples and bake test loaves,
because protein content alone does not

112
00:06:41,109 --> 00:06:44,043
reliably predict how a flour will perform.

113
00:06:44,043 --> 00:06:48,822
Flours with similar protein can make very
different bread, depending on how those

114
00:06:48,822 --> 00:06:49,648
proteins link.

115
00:06:49,648 --> 00:06:54,254
Research published through the Institute
of Food Technologists treats dough as a

116
00:06:54,254 --> 00:06:57,536
material to be characterised, not a recipe
to be trusted.

117
00:06:57,536 --> 00:07:01,467
There is one failure that looks like the
others but is really the reverse.

118
00:07:01,467 --> 00:07:06,318
Leave a dough to proof too long, and the
yeast keeps making gas after the gluten

119
00:07:06,318 --> 00:07:08,683
network has stretched as far as it can.

120
00:07:08,683 --> 00:07:13,659
The thinnest films tear, neighbouring gas
cells merge, and the structure sags.

121
00:07:13,659 --> 00:07:18,243
By the time you come back, an overproofed
dough is a dough that rose and then

122
00:07:18,243 --> 00:07:20,565
collapsed, not a dough that never rose.

123
00:07:20,565 --> 00:07:25,195
The yeast may also have used up much of
the available sugar, leaving little for

124
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:25,723
the oven.

125
00:07:25,723 --> 00:07:30,507
That oven phase is the last chance for
rise, and it explains why some loaves look

126
00:07:30,507 --> 00:07:32,928
fine on the counter and disappoint later.

127
00:07:32,928 --> 00:07:38,179
In the oven, gas cells expand with heat,
dissolved carbon dioxide comes out of

128
00:07:38,179 --> 00:07:42,757
solution, and ethanol and water turn to
vapour, all pushing outward.

129
00:07:42,757 --> 00:07:44,519
Bakers call this oven spring.

130
00:07:44,519 --> 00:07:49,621
It ends when the starch gelatinises and
the proteins set, fixing the crumb in

131
00:07:49,621 --> 00:07:54,160
A dough that has already exhausted its
network has nothing left to spring with.

132
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,924
So how does anyone know which half of the
rise failed?

133
00:07:57,924 --> 00:08:03,153
In a cereal laboratory, the job of
separating the two failures is handed to

134
00:08:03,153 --> 00:08:03,989
instruments.

135
00:08:03,989 --> 00:08:08,998
A rheofermentometer holds a dough in a
sealed chamber and tracks gas produced and

136
00:08:08,998 --> 00:08:14,131
gas retained at the same time: the total
carbon dioxide the yeast releases, and how

137
00:08:14,131 --> 00:08:16,357
much of it the dough actually holds.

138
00:08:16,357 --> 00:08:20,234
A sensor resting on the dough records
dough height over time.

139
00:08:20,234 --> 00:08:24,747
If production is high but retention is
low, the problem is the network.

140
00:08:24,747 --> 00:08:27,693
If both are low, the problem is the
fermenter.

141
00:08:27,693 --> 00:08:31,792
That separation is exactly what a home
kitchen cannot do by eye.

142
00:08:31,792 --> 00:08:34,155
Dough strength gets its own instrument.

143
00:08:34,155 --> 00:08:39,062
A farinograph mixes dough at a constant
speed and records the resistance of dough

144
00:08:39,062 --> 00:08:42,091
to mixing as the gluten develops and then
weakens.

145
00:08:42,091 --> 00:08:47,111
The curve shows how much water a flour
absorbs, how long it takes to develop, and

146
00:08:47,111 --> 00:08:48,784
how quickly it breaks down.

147
00:08:48,784 --> 00:08:51,218
It measures dough development, not gas.

148
00:08:51,218 --> 00:08:56,272
For the inside of the loaf, researchers
use X-ray microtomography, which builds a

149
00:08:56,272 --> 00:09:01,202
three-dimensional image of the crumb, and
magnetic resonance imaging, which can

150
00:09:01,202 --> 00:09:03,136
follow a dough while it proofs.

151
00:09:03,136 --> 00:09:10,050
Those images show gas cells growing,
merging and, in an overproofed dough,

152
00:09:10,050 --> 00:09:11,077
collapsing.

153
00:09:11,077 --> 00:09:15,445
Watch how the edit keeps returning to two
words: made and kept.

154
00:09:15,445 --> 00:09:19,205
Every graphic in this episode sits on one
side of that line.

155
00:09:19,205 --> 00:09:23,115
The checklist of heat, cold, salt and age
belongs to the fermenter.

156
00:09:23,115 --> 00:09:27,015
The gluten steps and the overproofing
contrast belong to the network.

157
00:09:27,015 --> 00:09:31,595
The instruments are shown last on purpose,
because they are the only thing in the

158
00:09:31,595 --> 00:09:35,835
story that can pull those two halves apart
and measure each one separately.

159
00:09:35,835 --> 00:09:39,165
Here is what this evidence settles, and
what it does not.

160
00:09:39,165 --> 00:09:44,015
Laboratory instruments separate gas made
from gas kept under controlled conditions,

161
00:09:44,015 --> 00:09:46,528
with measured flour, water and
temperature.

162
00:09:46,528 --> 00:09:51,690
A home kitchen varies all of them at once,
and failures often overlap: a slightly

163
00:09:51,690 --> 00:09:56,661
cool room, a weaker flour and a little
extra salt can together do what none of

164
00:09:56,661 --> 00:09:57,936
them would do alone.

165
00:09:57,936 --> 00:10:01,609
The evidence settles that gas must be made
and kept.

166
00:10:01,609 --> 00:10:05,141
It does not identify the cause of any
single loaf.

167
00:10:05,141 --> 00:10:09,520
Nor does it fix how fast stored yeast
declines, and published figures for yeast

168
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:13,456
tolerance vary enough that a single
threshold would be false precision.

169
00:10:13,456 --> 00:10:18,091
A flat dough is a record of two systems,
one alive and one structural.

170
00:10:18,091 --> 00:10:22,757
Before changing the recipe, it is worth
asking which of them gave out: the yeast

171
00:10:22,757 --> 00:10:26,256
that makes the gas, or the gluten that was
meant to hold it.
